# Schedule for the automated suspension job.
#
# Deliberately NOT live yet. This reports what would be suspended and changes
# nothing. Review the audit log for a few days, then add --live to the command
# and rebuild.
#
# The job runs as "provisioner", not root: cron itself runs as root because it
# has to be able to drop privileges to the job's user, and it cannot do that
# while running unprivileged (seteuid fails and the container crash-loops).
#
# m  h dom mon dow  user       command
# 17 past 2am, deliberately off the hour: other cron jobs on the host tend to
# fire at :00, and this one makes several calls to a slow billing portal.
#
# The timezone is needed twice, and one setting cannot do both.
#
# cron runs jobs in its own environment, not the one PID 1 was started with, so
# TZ=Asia/Thimphu from compose never reached the job: a `date` inside one
# printed "Mon Oct 5 10:03:01 UTC 2026" while the container itself said 15:50 +06.
#
# CRON_TZ decides WHEN this fires -- and it is read by cron itself, so it cannot
# be lost. Without it, "17 2" meant 02:17 UTC, which is 08:17 in Thimphu.
#
# TZ on the command decides what the job WRITES. Verified on this cron: CRON_TZ
# set alone still gave a job `date` of 10:22 UTC. So scheduling correctly while
# stamping records in UTC, and the audit trail would have mixed +06 from manual
# runs with +00:00 from the nightly one -- the exact six-hour disagreement the
# provisioner was previously fixed for.
CRON_TZ=Asia/Thimphu
17 2 * * * provisioner cd /app && TZ=Asia/Thimphu /opt/venv/bin/python scripts/suspend_expired.py >> /proc/1/fd/1 2>&1
